  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Boeing’s federal contract activity in FY2026 to date shows a concentrated, higher-volume year. Boeing obligated $9B on 543 awards, with average award size $16.6M, and obligations were up 106.1% year over year. The Department of Defense is the leading buyer, obligating $9B in the current fiscal year to date. Annual comparisons show past-year obligations of $7.3B in FY21, $3.9B in FY22, $3.3B in FY23, $5B in FY24, and $4.4B in FY25. Industry-level obligations are concentrated in Aircraft Manufacturing at $8.9B, followed by much smaller amounts in Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $67.8M and Computer Systems Design Services at $14.4M. Several NAICS lines show minimal or zero obligations, including Bolt, Nut, Screw, Rivet, and Washer Manufacturing at $0 and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $0. The data reflect contract obligations only, with the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables reported as total contract values rather than obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Boeing's FY2026 federal obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the primary buyer. The Department of Defense obligated $9B, which matches Boeing’s total obligations for the fiscal year to date. Other federal buyers show negligible or zero obligations in this dataset, for example the General Services Administration at $0. The largest industry concentration is Aircraft Manufacturing at $8.9B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where do FY2026 obligations fall by industry and award counts?",
        "body": "Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest industry by a wide margin at $8.9B. Other notable industry figures include Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $67.8M and Computer Systems Design Services at $14.4M. Boeing received 543 awards in FY2026 to date, with average award size $16.6M."
      }
    ]
  },
